## Part 1: Security & Authentication
Q1: How secure is the login system? What happens if someone tries to hack into an account?
A: The Falcon PMS authentication system implements multiple layers of security to protect user accounts.
First, we use JWT (JSON Web Tokens) with short lifespans - access tokens expire after just 30 minutes. This means even if a token were intercepted, it would become useless within half an hour. Refresh tokens are rotated every time they're used, so a stolen refresh token cannot be reused.
Second, we have automatic brute force protection. After 5 failed login attempts, the account is temporarily locked for 15 minutes. We also track login attempts by IP address, so if someone tries to attack multiple accounts from the same location, that IP gets blocked.
Third, we support Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA). Users can enable TOTP-based 2FA using Google Authenticator, Microsoft Authenticator, or any compatible app. When enabled, even if someone steals the password, they cannot access the account without the one-time code that changes every 30 seconds.
Fourth, all passwords are hashed using PBKDF2 with 100,000 iterations - the same standard used by major financial institutions. We never store plain-text passwords.
Fifth, we provide backup codes during MFA setup. If a user loses their phone, they have 10 one-time backup codes stored securely to regain access.
Finally, all login attempts are audited. You can see exactly when and from which IP address each login occurred, helping you detect suspicious activity.
________________________________________
Q2: Can we enforce MFA for certain users or departments?
A: Absolutely. The system provides granular MFA enforcement at the tenant level.
As a Client Administrator, you can configure which roles are required to use MFA. For example, you might require all executives and supervisors to enable MFA while making it optional for regular staff. You could also require it for finance department users or anyone accessing sensitive data.
The configuration is done through the Tenant Settings panel. You simply select which roles require MFA, and the system will enforce it automatically. Users in those roles will be prompted to set up MFA on their next login and will not be able to access the system until they complete the setup.
This is particularly useful for compliance requirements like SOC2, ISO 27001, or internal security policies that mandate MFA for privileged users.
________________________________________
Q3: What happens if an employee leaves the company? How do we revoke their access immediately?
A: You have multiple options to revoke access immediately:
First, you can deactivate the user's account. This takes effect instantly - the user cannot log in, and any active sessions are terminated immediately. The user's data remains in the system for audit purposes, but they cannot access anything.
Second, you can delete the user entirely. This removes their access and optionally anonymizes their historical data if required for compliance.
Third, you can terminate all active sessions for a user. This is useful if someone forgets to log out or if you suspect a session has been compromised.
As a Client Administrator, you can do any of these actions from the User Management panel with just a few clicks. For immediate termination of a departing employee, I recommend deactivating the account first, then processing the offboarding paperwork, and finally deciding whether to delete or archive the account.
All these actions are logged in the audit trail, so you have a record of when access was revoked and by whom.
________________________________________
Q4: Can users log in from multiple devices simultaneously?
A: Yes, users can log in from multiple devices, but you have control over this.
By default, the system allows up to 5 concurrent sessions per user. This is usually sufficient for most users who might have a desktop at work, a laptop at home, and a mobile phone.
If a user exceeds this limit, the oldest session is automatically terminated. Users can also see all their active sessions from the Security Settings page, showing the device type, browser, location, and last activity time for each session. They can terminate individual sessions remotely - for example, if they forgot to log out from a public computer.
As an administrator, you can adjust the maximum concurrent sessions limit in the tenant settings. You can also view all active sessions for any user and terminate them if needed. This is particularly useful for security audits or when investigating suspicious activity.
________________________________________
Q5: How does password reset work? Can users reset their own passwords?
A: Yes, users can reset their own passwords through a secure, automated process.
When a user clicks "Forgot Password," they enter their email address. The system generates a secure, time-limited token (expires in 1 hour) and sends a reset link to their registered email. The link takes them to a secure page where they can create a new password.
The reset token is one-time use and cannot be guessed or forged. We never send the new password via email - only a secure link.
As an administrator, you can also force a password reset for any user. This is useful for onboarding new employees, resetting compromised accounts, or enforcing password policies. When you force a reset, the user receives an email with a reset link on their next login attempt.
All password changes, whether initiated by the user or forced by an administrator, are logged in the audit trail with the IP address and timestamp.
