Part 2: User Management & Roles
________________________________________
Q6: What are the different user roles and what can each do?
A: The system has seven predefined roles with clear, hierarchical permissions:
Super Admin - Complete system-wide access. Can manage all tenants, users, and system settings. Typically only 1-2 people in your organization would have this role, primarily for platform maintenance.
Client Admin - Full control over their organization's tenant. Can create and manage users, assign roles, configure branding, manage subscriptions, and view audit logs. This is typically your HR manager or IT administrator.
Executive - Strategic oversight without administrative privileges. Can view all users, team hierarchies, and organization-wide reports. Cannot create or modify users. This is for C-suite and department heads.
Supervisor - Manages a team. Can view their team members' profiles, KPIs, and performance data. Can approve or reject KPI entries submitted by their team. Cannot see data outside their team.
Dashboard Champion - Specialized role for entering company-wide targets and monitoring aggregate performance. Can phase targets across months, cascade goals to departments, and track submission compliance.
Staff - Regular employee. Can only see and edit their own profile, KPIs, and tasks. Cannot see other users' data.
Read Only - View-only access. Can see dashboards and reports but cannot make any changes or submit data. Perfect for auditors, consultants, or temporary reviewers.
Each role inherits permissions from higher roles, and you can also create custom roles with specific permission combinations if the predefined roles don't exactly match your needs.
________________________________________
Q7: Can we create custom roles? How does that work?
A: Yes, absolutely. While the seven predefined roles cover most common scenarios, you can create custom roles tailored to your organization's specific structure.
Creating a custom role is straightforward. As a Client Administrator, you go to the Roles section, click "Create Role," and give it a name and description. Then you select permissions from a categorized list - everything from "view users" to "approve KPI changes" to "export reports."
You can also set a parent role, and the new role will inherit all permissions from its parent. This is efficient because you only need to add the additional permissions that make this role unique.
For example, you might create a "Regional Manager" role that inherits from Supervisor but adds the ability to view data across multiple departments. Or a "Compliance Officer" role that inherits from Read Only but adds audit log viewing.
Custom roles are assignable by default, meaning your administrators can assign them to users. System roles (like Super Admin) are not assignable and cannot be deleted to maintain system integrity.
________________________________________
Q8: How does the reporting hierarchy work? Can we model complex organizational structures?
A: The system supports unlimited hierarchical depth, so you can model any organizational structure, no matter how complex.
Each user has a "manager" field that points to their direct supervisor. This creates a tree structure. A supervisor can see all their direct reports, and those reports' reports, all the way down the chain. There's no limit on depth - you can model a flat team of 3 people or a global enterprise with 10 layers of management.
You can also assign users to departments, and departments can have their own hierarchies. This gives you two parallel structures: the reporting chain (who reports to whom) and the departmental structure (which team or division someone belongs to).
The system automatically handles cross-department reporting. If a matrix organization has someone reporting to a manager in a different department, that's fully supported.
The team dashboard for a supervisor shows their entire team tree, and they can click through any member to see that person's complete details, including their own team if they're also a manager.
________________________________________
Q9: How do we invite new users? Can we invite multiple at once?
A: Inviting users is simple and flexible.
You can invite users individually by entering their email address, selecting their role, and optionally adding a personal message. The system sends them an email with a secure invitation link that expires after 7 days. When they click the link, they're guided through account creation, where they set their password and complete their profile.
For bulk invitations, you can upload a CSV file with multiple email addresses and their assigned roles. The system processes the file and sends invitations to everyone in one batch. This is perfect for onboarding a new department or team.
You can also resend invitations if someone didn't receive theirs, and you can cancel pending invitations if a user was invited by mistake or no longer needs access.
All invitations are tracked, so you can see who has accepted, who hasn't, and which invitations are still pending.
