Part 3: Session & Activity Monitoring
________________________________________
Q10: How can we see who is logged in and what they're doing?
A: The system provides comprehensive visibility into user activity at multiple levels.
Individual users can see their own active sessions from the Security Settings page. They can see which devices they're logged in on, when they logged in, and from which IP address. They can terminate any session remotely - very useful if they suspect unauthorized access.
As a Supervisor, you can see your team members' active sessions. As a Client Administrator, you can see all active sessions for your entire organization.
For historical activity, the Audit Logs provide a complete record of every significant action. You can filter by user, action type (login, create, update, delete), severity (info, warning, error, critical), date range, and even IP address.
You can see exactly who created a user, who changed a role, who viewed sensitive data, and when. This is invaluable for security audits and compliance reporting.
The audit logs are immutable - once written, they cannot be modified or deleted. This ensures the integrity of your audit trail for regulatory compliance.
________________________________________
Q11: What is session timeout? Can we configure it?
A: Yes, session timeout is fully configurable at the tenant level.
Session timeout determines how long a user can remain inactive before being automatically logged out. By default, it's set to 8 hours (480 minutes), which is appropriate for most office environments where users stay logged in throughout the workday.
You can adjust this in the Tenant Settings. For high-security environments, you might set it to 30 minutes. For a less restrictive environment, you could set it to 12 hours or even disable it entirely (not recommended for security).
When a session times out, the user is logged out and must re-authenticate. Any unsaved work would be lost, so we recommend setting a timeout that balances security with user convenience.
The system also shows users a warning before timeout, typically 5 minutes before, giving them a chance to extend their session with a single click.
________________________________________
Q12: Can we see failed login attempts? How do we handle locked accounts?
A: Yes, the system tracks all login attempts - successful and failed.
Failed login attempts are visible in the audit logs. You can filter by "failure" result to see only failed attempts. For each failure, you'll see the email attempted, the IP address, the timestamp, and the reason (wrong password, account locked, MFA failed, etc.).
When an account is locked due to too many failed attempts (5 failures in 15 minutes), you have two options:
First, the account automatically unlocks after 15 minutes. This is the default behavior and works for most cases where someone simply forgot their password.
Second, as an administrator, you can manually unlock the account immediately. This is useful if a legitimate user is locked out and needs access urgently.
You can also adjust the lockout threshold in the system settings - for example, 3 failures in 10 minutes for high-security environments, or 10 failures in 30 minutes for less restrictive environments.
