Part 4: Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA)
________________________________________
Q13: What MFA methods are supported?
A: The system primarily supports TOTP (Time-based One-Time Password), which is the industry standard used by Google Authenticator, Microsoft Authenticator, Authy, and most other authenticator apps.
TOTP is our recommended method because it works offline, doesn't require SMS or email delivery, and is widely supported across all devices and platforms.
The system also supports backup codes - 10 one-time use codes generated during MFA setup. These are essential for account recovery if a user loses access to their authenticator app.
For organizations requiring additional methods, we can integrate SMS or email-based MFA as custom development. This sends a one-time code via text message or email, but note these methods have delivery dependencies and are slightly less secure than TOTP.
Hardware tokens like YubiKey can also be integrated for organizations with higher security requirements.
________________________________________
Q14: What happens if a user loses their phone with the authenticator app?
A: This is a common concern, and we have a straightforward recovery process.
When a user sets up MFA, the system generates 10 backup codes. We strongly recommend users save these codes in a secure location - printed and kept in a safe, stored in a password manager, or saved on a secure company drive.
If a user loses their phone, they can use one of their backup codes to log in. Each backup code works once. After using a backup code, they can set up MFA again on their new device, which generates new backup codes.
If a user has lost both their phone AND their backup codes, as an administrator you can reset their MFA configuration. You would do this from the User Management panel, selecting the user and choosing "Reset MFA." The user will then be prompted to set up MFA again on their next login.
This operation is logged in the audit trail, so you have a record of when and why MFA was reset.
________________________________________
Q15: Can we require MFA only for certain actions, not just login?
A: Currently, MFA is enforced at login. Once a user authenticates with MFA, their session is considered trusted for its duration.
However, for high-security operations (like changing roles, accessing audit logs, or modifying tenant settings), you can configure step-up authentication. This would require the user to re-verify their MFA even within an active session.
This is a configurable option in the tenant settings. You can specify which actions require step-up authentication. For example, you might require MFA re-verification for role assignments but not for viewing dashboards.
This balances security with user convenience - casual browsing doesn't require repeated MFA, but sensitive operations trigger an additional verification.
