Part 5: Audit & Compliance
________________________________________
Q16: What gets logged in the audit trail? How long do we keep logs?
A: The audit trail captures every significant action in the system:
•	Authentication events - Login attempts (success and failure), logout, password changes, MFA setup and verification
•	User management - User creation, updates, deletion, role assignments, invitations
•	Data modifications - Creation, update, and deletion of any record (KPIs, reviews, missions, etc.)
•	Access events - When someone views sensitive data like audit logs or user profiles
•	Administrative actions - Tenant configuration changes, system setting modifications
For each event, we log:
•	Who performed the action (user email)
•	What action was performed
•	When it happened (timestamp)
•	Where it came from (IP address)
•	What changed (old value and new value for updates)
•	Severity level (info, warning, error, critical)
Audit logs are retained for 365 days by default. You can configure this retention period in the tenant settings - anywhere from 90 days to 7 years, depending on your compliance requirements.
After the retention period, logs are automatically archived or deleted. For organizations with strict compliance needs, we can configure archiving to external storage (AWS S3, Azure Blob) for long-term retention.
