Part 6: Performance & Scalability
________________________________________
Q19: How many users can the system handle? Does it scale?
A: The system is designed for enterprise-scale deployment.
The architecture supports horizontal scaling - you can add more application servers as your user base grows. The database can be scaled with read replicas, connection pooling, and sharding if needed.
In standard configuration, a single server can comfortably handle:
•	5,000 to 10,000 concurrent users
•	50,000 to 100,000 total user accounts
•	Hundreds of thousands of daily API requests
With proper scaling (load balancers, multiple app servers, database replicas), the system can support millions of users across thousands of tenants.
The multi-tenant architecture ensures that one organization's activity doesn't impact another's performance. Each tenant's data is isolated, and we can prioritize resources for enterprise tenants.
For specific capacity planning, we can run load tests based on your expected user count and usage patterns to determine the optimal infrastructure configuration.
________________________________________
Q20: Is the system available 24/7? What about maintenance?
A: We target 99.9% uptime for the SaaS version, with maintenance windows scheduled during off-peak hours.
For on-premise deployments, availability depends on your infrastructure, but the application itself is designed for high availability.
Zero-downtime deployments - We use blue-green deployment strategies, meaning new versions are deployed alongside the current version, and traffic is switched over seamlessly. Users don't experience interruption during updates.
Database migrations - We design migrations to be backward-compatible and non-locking, so the database remains available during schema changes.
Maintenance windows - When downtime is unavoidable (rare), we notify administrators at least 7 days in advance and schedule during low-activity periods (typically weekends, 2 AM - 6 AM local time).
Health monitoring - The system includes built-in health checks that monitor database connectivity, cache availability, and queue processing. Administrators receive alerts if any component degrades.
Disaster recovery - Regular automated backups and cross-region replication ensure data can be restored quickly if needed.
