Part 28: Additional Security Questions
________________________________________
Q27: How are API keys managed? Can we rotate them?
A: The system uses JWT (JSON Web Tokens) for API authentication, which are short-lived by design.
Access tokens expire after 30 minutes by default. Refresh tokens can live longer (7 days) and are rotated on each use. This means API keys (tokens) are constantly changing, reducing the risk of stolen tokens.
For system-to-system integration, we support API keys that can be generated, revoked, and rotated by administrators. Each API key can be restricted to specific IP addresses and specific permission scopes.
You can rotate secrets from the Admin panel, which immediately invalidates all existing tokens and requires users to log in again. This is useful during security incidents or as part of regular security maintenance.
All API key operations are logged in the audit trail, including generation, usage, and revocation.
________________________________________
Q28: Is the data encrypted in the database?
A: Yes, we implement encryption at multiple levels.
At Rest - The database supports transparent data encryption (TDE), encrypting all data files. Additionally, sensitive fields like MFA secrets are encrypted at the application level using AES-256.
In Transit - All communication between the browser and server, and between services, uses TLS 1.3 encryption. API endpoints require HTTPS.
Backups - Database backups are encrypted before being stored.
Key Management - Encryption keys are stored separately from the data, in a dedicated key management system (KMS). Keys can be rotated regularly.
Field-Level Encryption - Specific sensitive fields (passwords, MFA secrets) are hashed or encrypted individually, providing an additional layer of protection.
For on-premise deployments, you can provide your own encryption keys, ensuring that even we cannot access your data.
